/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  relief01_ammonia_loop_gas -- THE GAS SIDE OF A BLOCKED-OUTLET RELIEF VALVE
  ON THE AMMONIA SYNTHESIS LOOP (DEV.md 4c C61, the "Designing for
  overpressure" EduTool).

  THE QUESTION.  The converter's outlet is blocked while the recycle
  compressor goes on delivering the converter feed.  The relief valve on
  the feed side must pass that whole flow at the relieving pressure, and
  the gas relief equation needs two properties of the gas there besides
  its molar mass: the compressibility factor Z and the ratio of specific
  heats k = Cp/Cv.  This case computes both, for the same loop at 150 bar
  and at 50 bar -- the two green-ammonia feasibility cases the
  Process-safety EduTool compares (tutorials/plant/greenAmmonia02_feasibility150bar
  and greenAmmonia02_feasibility50bar).

  WHERE THE STATES COME FROM.
    * composition -- each plant's converged converter feed (`PreheatedFeed`),
      copied as the component flows in kmol/h exactly as the plant's
      converged/PreheatedFeed prints them.  propertyPoint normalises the
      composition, so the flows ARE the composition; the EduTool reads them
      again as the relieving rate.  gui/tests/overpressure.test.ts holds
      every number to the plant's own converged file.
    * T -- the same stream's temperature (653.15 K and 593.15 K): a blocked
      outlet relieves the gas as it arrives.
    * P -- the RELIEVING pressure, worked from the stream's operating
      pressure in the EduTool and written here to the digit:
        operating (gauge)  = P_stream - 1.01325 bar
        design (gauge)     = max(1.1 x operating, operating + 1.7 bar)
                             (the Design Guide's rule of thumb for a
                             PRELIMINARY design pressure, docs/designGuide.tex,
                             "Pressure relief, flare & safety basics")
        MAWP (gauge)       = design (gauge) -- an ASSUMPTION, the lowest
                             MAWP the vessel can have; a real MAWP is a
                             property of the built vessel, never of a rule
                             of thumb
        set pressure       = MAWP (a single device)
        relieving (gauge)  = max(1.10 x MAWP, MAWP + 3 psi)
        relieving (abs)    = relieving (gauge) + 1.01325 bar
      The design pressure is a rule of thumb applied to a feasibility case
      that sizes no vessel, and the MAWP is assumed equal to it; no number
      here is the design pressure or the MAWP of any real loop.

  WHAT IS COMPUTED.  propertyPoint's base set: Z on the SRK vapour root,
  and gamma = Cp_ig/(Cp_ig - R) from the records' ideal-gas heat capacities
  -- the ideal-gas ratio the gas relief equation is derived with.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
operations
(
    {
        name        loop150;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state
        {
            T            653.15 K;
            P            18212635.5767 Pa;
            composition  { N2 4182.558405;  H2 14990.08692;
                           NH3 350.9093071;  Ar 43.92316744; }
        }
    }
    {
        name        loop50;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state
        {
            T            593.15 K;
            P            6021971.3705 Pa;
            composition  { N2 6867.069031;  H2 22660.88369;
                           NH3 1292.693628;  Ar 107.0317684; }
        }
    }
);
